exports[`screen: 'help wordpress update plugin' > en 40 plain 1`] = `
"  bladecp wordpress update plugin
  <server> <plugin>
 ──────────────────────────────────────
  Asks BladeCP to update one plugin to
  its newest version. On a terminal it
  asks first. With --wait it follows
  the update to the end.

  Usage ────────────────────────────────
  bladecp wordpress update plugin
      <server> <plugin>
      [--external-id <id>] [--wait]
      [--yes]

  Arguments ───────────────────────────
  <server>   The server: its ID, or its
             first characters (at least
             4).
  <plugin>   The plugin as WordPress
             names it (its slug), for
             example akismet.

  Options ─────────────────────────────
  --external-id <id>
    Partners: ID of the service in your
    billing system
  --wait
    Wait until the update ends and show
    how it went
  -y, --yes
    Do not ask before updating on a
    terminal

  On your own servers nothing else is
  needed. With a partner key, add
  --external-id: the ID of the service
  in your billing system. BladeCP
  checks that it matches the server.

  Examples ────────────────────────────
  › bladecp wordpress update plugin
    0197a1c4 akismet --yes
    Update a plugin without the
    question

  Exit codes ──────────────────────────
    0 done (with --wait: the task
      ended well)
    1 with --wait: the task failed,
      took too long or was cancelled

  Global options ──────────────────────
  --json
    Print machine-readable JSON (no
    colors, no prompts)
  --api-url <url>
    API address (default
    https://api.bladecp.com)
  --lang <en|pt-BR|es>
    Language for messages
  --debug
    Describe each request on stderr
    (the key is never shown)
